# Simple features: ggsf

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_sf()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/ggsf.html), which covers `coord_sf()`,
`geom_sf_label()`, `geom_sf_text()` and `stat_sf()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each
example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its
ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

These functions draw simple features: `sf` objects, from R's `sf` package, tables whose
`geometry` column holds points, lines or polygons. `geom_sf()` draws whichever of those each row
holds, through `stat_sf()`, and adds `coord_sf()`, which projects every layer into one coordinate
reference system (CRS): the one `crs` names, or else the first layer's. `geom_sf_text()` and
`geom_sf_label()` write text at a point on each geometry. Giggly has none of it yet: it has no
`sf` objects, and draws in Cartesian or radial coordinates only.

## Counties filled by area

The examples read the counties of North Carolina from a shapefile that comes with the `sf`
package, and run only where that package is installed.

```r
if (requireNamespace("sf", quietly = TRUE)) {
nc <- sf::st_read(system.file("shape/nc.shp", package = "sf"), quiet = TRUE)
ggplot(nc) +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = AREA))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects and `geom_sf()`.

## Layers in different projections

```r
# If not supplied, coord_sf() will take the CRS from the first layer
# and automatically transform all other layers to use that CRS. This
# ensures that all data will correctly line up
nc_3857 <- sf::st_transform(nc, 3857)
ggplot() +
  geom_sf(data = nc) +
  geom_sf(data = nc_3857, colour = "red", fill = NA)
```

`sf::st_transform()` projects the counties into another CRS, Web Mercator (EPSG code 3857), and
`coord_sf()` projects them back into the first layer's, so the red outlines fall on the counties
beneath them.

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects, `geom_sf()`, `coord_sf()`, and a plot with no data.

## Points among polygons

```r
# Unfortunately if you plot other types of feature you'll need to use
# show.legend to tell ggplot2 what type of legend to use
nc_3857$mid <- sf::st_centroid(nc_3857$geometry)
ggplot(nc_3857) +
  geom_sf(colour = "white") +
  geom_sf(aes(geometry = mid, size = AREA), show.legend = "point")
```

`sf::st_centroid()` finds the centre of each county, which the second layer draws as a point.

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects, `geom_sf()` and its `show.legend` option, and mapping `geometry`.

## Longitude and latitude in other layers

```r
# You can also use layers with x and y aesthetics. To have these interpreted
# as longitude/latitude you need to set the default CRS in coord_sf()
ggplot(nc_3857) +
  geom_sf() +
  annotate("point", x = -80, y = 35, colour = "red", size = 4) +
  coord_sf(default_crs = sf::st_crs(4326))
```

EPSG code 4326 is longitude and latitude, so the red point is at 80° west, 35° north.

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects, `geom_sf()`, and `coord_sf()` and its `default_crs` option.

## Labels

```r
# To add labels, use geom_sf_label().
ggplot(nc_3857[1:3, ]) +
   geom_sf(aes(fill = AREA)) +
   geom_sf_label(aes(label = NAME))
}
```

Not in Giggly yet: `sf` objects, and `geom_sf()` and `geom_sf_label()`.

## The world in two projections

These examples need map data from the `maps` package, and run only where it is installed.

```r
# Thanks to the power of sf, a geom_sf nicely handles varying projections
# setting the aspect ratio correctly.
if (requireNamespace('maps', quietly = TRUE)) {
library(maps)
world1 <- sf::st_as_sf(map('world', plot = FALSE, fill = TRUE))
ggplot() + geom_sf(data = world1)

world2 <- sf::st_transform(
  world1,
  "+proj=laea +y_0=0 +lon_0=155 +lat_0=-90 +ellps=WGS84 +no_defs"
)
ggplot() + geom_sf(data = world2)
}
```

The second plot projects the world onto a Lambert azimuthal equal-area projection centred on the
South Pole.

Not in Giggly yet: map data from the `maps` package, `sf` objects, `geom_sf()`, and a plot with no
data.
